{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity"],"declarations":11,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.not_censusAffine_of_thirdDiff_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.not_censusAffine_of_thirdDiff_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":169,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.lean#L169-L176","doc":"Contrapositive, and the form the probe actually uses: a functional with a nonzero\nthird difference in `Z` is **not** an affine function of the all-pairs bond census —\nit carries a correlation the census cannot express. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.census_thirdDiff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.census_thirdDiff","kind":"theorem","line":162,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.lean#L162-L167","doc":"**The discriminator.** The third difference of an all-pairs census functional vanishes\nidentically: such a functional is exactly quadratic in `Z`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.census_secondDiff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.census_secondDiff","kind":"theorem","line":153,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.lean#L153-L160","doc":"The second difference of an all-pairs census functional is the **constant**\n`2·(f_pp + f_nn − 2 f_pn)`. In particular it is nonzero in general — so on this layout a\nnonzero second difference in `Z` is supplied by the *layout*, and is not evidence of a\ntwo-bond correlation in the functional. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.census","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.census","kind":"def","line":143,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.lean#L143-L151","doc":"**Twice** the all-pairs bond census functional, doubled so that `C(Z,2) = Z(Z−1)/2`\nneeds no division and the statement holds over any commutative ring:\n\n    2·F(Z) = f_pp·Z(Z−1) + 2·f_pn·Z(A−Z) + f_nn·(A−Z)(A−Z−1) + c.\n\n`c` absorbs everything constant in `Z` (in the UIC that is the per-nucleon intra-nucleon\npart, which is `A` letters regardless of how many are protons). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.fwd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.fwd","kind":"def","line":140,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.lean#L140-L141","doc":"Forward difference in the proton number, step 1. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.twoCrossing_mul_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.twoCrossing_mul_iff","kind":"theorem","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.lean#L123-L130","doc":"The block trace is multiplicative over two bonds **iff** the defect vanishes; under\nthe `wM·(x−y)² = 1` normalisation that happens exactly when `wP = 0`, i.e. when the block\nhas only one live branch. So a genuinely two-branch block is never multiplicative. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.twoCrossing_defect_eq_wPlus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.twoCrossing_defect_eq_wPlus","kind":"theorem","line":114,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.lean#L114-L121","doc":"Under the normalisation `wM·(x−y)² = 1` the defect is exactly the plus-branch weight\n`wP`. Convention D satisfies it at `x = q`, `y = −q⁻¹`, `wM = 1/([3]_q + 1)`, because\n`(q + q⁻¹)² = q² + 2 + q⁻² = [3]_q + 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.twoCrossing_defect_offset_independent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.twoCrossing_defect_offset_independent","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.lean#L105-L112","doc":"The defect does not depend on the bond offsets at all. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.twoCrossing_defect","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.twoCrossing_defect","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.lean#L89-L103","doc":"**The exact non-multiplicativity defect.** For branch weights summing to one,\n\n    twoCrossing − oneCrossing(d₁)·oneCrossing(d₂) = wP · wM · (x − y)².\n\nThe right-hand side mentions **neither offset**: the failure of multiplicativity is the\nsame for every pair of bonds. This is the mechanism by which a per-block logarithm leaves\nthe additive class of `GradedBindingFunctionalPairTerm.additive_census`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.twoCrossing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.twoCrossing","kind":"def","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.lean#L82-L87","doc":"The trace of **two** crossings on the *same* generator, offsets `d₁`, `d₂`. Within\neach branch the eigenvalues multiply, so the two branches are summed *after* multiplying,\nnot before — which is exactly why this is not the product of the two one-crossing\ntraces. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.oneCrossing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.oneCrossing","kind":"def","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/UICPerBlockLogPairTerm.lean#L75-L80","doc":"The trace of **one** crossing with offset `d` in a two-branch block: the two\neigenvalues `x`, `y` are shifted by `d` and weighted by `wP`, `wM`.\n\nAt `x = q`, `y = −q⁻¹` this is `ChainTraceFactor.twoStrandTrace`, which equals `z + d`\nfor the convention-D Markov parameter `z = q³/(q²+1)`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}